From 2ad96eaf172b244675b6246e7161356ed34a393e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:58:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dialogs, estuary, settings: Kodi's original player
 information as a setting

Hades shows its own pages in the player information of Estuary (DialogPlayerProcessInfo.xml): the
overview, the path of the signal, the picture, the sound and Dolby Vision. Kodi's own panel, one
short panel with the decoders, the picture, the sound and the system, was no longer there to choose,
where two other builds let their users go back to it.

Now System > Display has "Kodi's original player information" (videoscreen.hadeskodiplayerinfo,
advanced level, off). On, the dialog loads DialogPlayerProcessInfoKodi.xml, Kodi 22.0 RC2's own
DialogPlayerProcessInfo.xml unchanged under a name of its own (its variables and strings checked to
be the same in the Estuary of Hades), in place of the pages of Hades
(CGUIDialogPlayerProcessInfo::AllocResources()). As with any skin whose dialog has no pages of
Hades, the second press of the player information opens them in their own dialog
(CGUIDialogPlayerProcessInfo::OnAction()), so nothing of them is out of reach. A skin without that
file keeps its own dialog whatever the setting says.

The dialog loads whichever file it is given as the windows loaded ahead do (CWindowPreload): while
the menus are idle, and, with the setting on when the skin loads, the pages of Hades in their own
dialog too, for the second press. After the setting changed, the dialog is read once at its next
open, which kodi.log says ("the player information is Kodi's own from now on
(DialogPlayerProcessInfoKodi.xml), loaded again").

Checked in the test machine (Kodi of the image with this branch's Kodi, settings and strings): the
first press shows Kodi's panel, the second the pages of Hades, and the setting turned off again
brings the pages of Hades back at the first press.

Strings: core #42200-42201, Estuary #34200-34201 (en_gb, es_es, es_ar, es_mx).
